1
00:00:00,404 --> 00:00:02,228
AI agents can finally buy things.

2
00:00:02,228 --> 00:00:04,964
The hard question is not whether they can pay.

3
00:00:04,964 --> 00:00:08,004
It is whether you would let one spend your money.

4
00:00:08,907 --> 00:00:09,592
Meet Bursar.

5
00:00:09,592 --> 00:00:12,675
A workspace opens in one click on PayPal's sandbox.

6
00:00:12,675 --> 00:00:15,758
The owner sets a mandate: a cap of $10,000.

7
00:00:15,758 --> 00:00:18,498
00, a limit per mission, and a window.

8
00:00:18,498 --> 00:00:22,951
It is tied to a PayPal Vault token, so revoking it is real.

9
00:00:23,854 --> 00:00:27,790
A mission states a goal and a budget of $1,000.

10
00:00:27,790 --> 00:00:28,184
00.

11
00:00:28,184 --> 00:00:36,844
The Planner splits it into 4 needs, and a researcher per need searches real products through tools that cannot name a price.

12
00:00:36,844 --> 00:00:42,355
Bursar re-quotes every offer and proposes one cart, priced by the server at $251.

13
00:00:42,355 --> 00:00:42,749
96.

14
00:00:42,749 --> 00:00:45,111
The model never types an amount.

15
00:00:46,012 --> 00:00:47,052
Nothing moves yet.

16
00:00:47,052 --> 00:00:52,945
17 pure rules rule on the cart, and one wants a person, because the supplier is new.

17
00:00:52,945 --> 00:00:55,025
The owner approves this exact cart.

18
00:00:55,025 --> 00:01:00,225
The approval is signed over its hash, so change one line and it is void.

19
00:01:00,225 --> 00:01:01,958
Then PayPal places the hold.

20
00:01:02,861 --> 00:01:10,599
Every action ends in a receipt: the rules that ran, who approved, what was sent to PayPal, and a tamper-evident audit chain.

21
00:01:10,599 --> 00:01:13,765
Replay the ruling, and it reproduces, byte for byte.

22
00:01:14,666 --> 00:01:15,877
Now an attack.

23
00:01:15,877 --> 00:01:20,317
Product titles are written by sellers, so they can carry instructions.

24
00:01:20,317 --> 00:01:24,353
27 injection payloads, read by the same gullible agent twice.

25
00:01:24,353 --> 00:01:29,600
With raw payment tools it is talked into paying or ordering 24 times.

26
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:32,425
Through Bursar, it makes 0 PayPal calls.

27
00:01:33,328 --> 00:01:37,165
Before any agent runs, Bursar attacks its own policy.

28
00:01:37,165 --> 00:01:43,133
Remove the daily limit, and the lab finds 6 ways through, spending about $3,424.

29
00:01:43,133 --> 00:01:45,264
47 with no person involved.

30
00:01:45,264 --> 00:01:51,232
It shrinks one to the fewest orders that still works, and proposes the fix.

31
00:01:57,238 --> 00:02:03,064
The cockpit shows the whole workspace at once: the envelope, which rules fire, and where the money went.

32
00:02:03,064 --> 00:02:07,595
Ask the Treasurer in plain words and it answers from figures the server summed.

33
00:02:07,595 --> 00:02:09,861
It can read, and it can chart.

34
00:02:09,861 --> 00:02:11,156
It cannot move money.

35
00:02:14,105 --> 00:02:14,830
Reality changes.

36
00:02:14,830 --> 00:02:18,090
A carrier says a delivery is 3 days late.

37
00:02:18,090 --> 00:02:23,162
The scheduler finds the fewest swaps that still meet the deadline, and proposes them.

38
00:02:23,162 --> 00:02:25,698
A recovery is only ever a proposal.

39
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:29,016
And if something bypasses Bursar entirely?

40
00:02:29,016 --> 00:02:33,043
A capture appears at PayPal with no decision behind it.

41
00:02:33,043 --> 00:02:42,304
The signed webhook arrives, nothing explains it, and within thirty seconds the Verifier freezes the mandate, refunds the charge, and revokes the token.

42
00:02:43,205 --> 00:02:43,673
Bursar.

43
00:02:43,673 --> 00:02:50,221
Spend authority for AI agents: governed by code, bound by PayPal, verified by webhooks.

44
00:02:50,221 --> 00:02:53,963
Sandbox only, and honest about what is simulated.

